{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>And you shall not lack salt.</b> Because it is a thing that endures, to make known that the offerings are an eternal covenant for atonement and not for the need of the Holy One, blessed be He — for there is before Him neither eating nor drinking, but only to merit Israel through them. For when a person sins and brings an offering and attains atonement, and knows that he is clean of his sin, he is careful afterward not to sin and not to become defiled again in transgression. But if he were not forgiven he would continue to sin, as the Sages said: once a person commits a transgression and repeats it, it becomes permitted to him — do you think it is actually permitted? Rather, it becomes like a permitted thing to him. Like a person whose garments are filthy with dung — he does not guard them from mud; but a person whose garments are clean and fine guards them from all filth. And of this Solomon said: ״בכל עת יהיו בגדיך לבנים״. And likewise the Sages said: the Place desired to merit Israel, therefore He multiplied for them Torah and commandments, so they would not defile themselves with sin. And He also commanded many commandments that by strict law a person would guard on his own, yet the Holy One, blessed be He, commanded them in order to multiply the reward of Israel."], :book :leviticus, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:07:50.649483011Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}